a typical italian olive farm struggling  in a period of atypical weather  unpredictable and extreme weather is  becoming more common making it  increasingly difficult for farmers like  mario cascone  in 2018 an exceptionally late frost left  him with little choice  the farm lost 40 percent of its trees  that day many others required drastic  action  this is a clear example of the direct  effects of climate change that little  tree we see there was the same size as  the big tree next to it before we had to  cut it back because it was completely  frozen it'd be as big as the other tree  if that hadn't happened  newly regrown trees give mario hope  still earnings are down because they  produce less oil  olives are one of italy's most iconic  staples the fact that they're under  threat has shown italians that climate  change has arrived in full force here  as have repeated floods in places like  venice record heat waves raging  wildfires and extended periods of  drought  surrounded by warming seas and at  growing risk of desertification italy  now finds itself on the front lines of  climate change  heavily reliant on agriculture italy is  hoping to take the lead in adapting to a  changing climate  the government has launched a new  ministry for ecological transition and  earmarked 80 billion dollars of eu  coveted recovery funds to come up with  solutions to live in a warming world  environmental transition has significant  costs and it is essential that  governments make direct commitments to  help their citizens and businesses  handle them italy has partnered with the  uk to host this year's u.n climate  summit cop 26 coming up in november in  scotland  and this week milan is hosting two  events ahead of those meetings the youth  for climate conference and the final  ministerial meetings before cop 26.  at 30 years old casconi could pick a  different career but he is drawn to the  challenge ahead  we need to be creative and open to  adaptation it's not easy it's hard work  but at the same time it can be  fulfilling  on the front lines the desire to solve  the problem is there but will world  leaders take the tough decisions to make  that possible adam rainey al jazeera  caneto italy
 

